A repository (repo) is a storage location where we store and manage our project files, source code, scripts, and configuration files.

In simple words, a repository is like a folder that stores all our project files along with their version history.
Example
Suppose you are developing a project called Expense Tracker.

You create a Git repository named expense-tracker.

Inside the repository, you can store:

expense-tracker/
│
├── server.js
├── package.json
├── README.md
├── config/
├── scripts/
└── .git/

Why do we need a repository?
1. Code Storage: Stores all project source code in one place.
2. Version Control: Tracks code changes over time.
3. Collaboration: Multiple developers can work on the same project.
4. Backup: Remote repositories provide an additional copy of the code.
5. Tracking: Helps identify who made changes and when.
6. Rollback: Allows us to return to an earlier version of the code.
Types of repositories
There are two main types:
Type	             Explanation
Local Repository	Repository available on our local computer.
Remote Repository	Repository hosted on platforms like GitHub, GitLab, or Bitbucket.

Important Git commands

# Create a repository
git init

# Check repository status
git status

# Add files
git add .

# Commit changes
git commit -m "Initial commit"

# Connect remote repository
git remote add origin <repository-url>

# Push code to remote repository
git push origin main
